{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a downtrend (e.g., falling from ~76 to ~62) followed by a model forecast that assumes immediate recovery or linear growth, apply a strong downward correction bias because true outcomes often continue the downtrend for several days before reversing.",
    "If sentiment history contains a single -1.00 spike amidst neutral values (0.00), the model should not overreact with an immediate upward trend; instead, reduce forecasted gains by 5-8% to account for delayed market reaction.",
    "In volatile regimes where true outcomes exhibit high frequency spikes and drops (e.g., Example 5), avoid linear extrapolation of the mean; switch to a mean-reverting strategy that weights recent volatility higher than long-term averages.",
    "When the model forecast aligns with the last known price but fails to capture subsequent volatility expansion, reduce confidence in the forecast horizon by assuming wider dispersion around the trend line.",
    "If sentiment remains neutral (0.00) for 15+ days while prices are trending down, assume the downtrend persists longer than the model predicts and apply a negative correction factor to the forecasted path."
  ]
}
